  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 150 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nWe begin the weekend on a warm and dry note as the primary surge of \nmonsoonal moisture lifts off to the north and east in conjunction \nwith the ejecting shortwave trough that has the chance to bring us \nsome beneficial moisture the day prior. This will result in well \nabove-average temperatures during the day, with forecast highs \nanticipated to be 5-10 degrees warmer than climatology. For \nreference, the average high for Cheyenne on September 26 is 68F \n(yes, we are down into the 60s for averages now!) and 75F for \nScottsbluff. With the absence of any forcing throughout the day \nthanks to the building ridge and 500 mb height rises, expect dry \nconditions. Fire concerns are minimal at this time as 25th \npercentile afternoon RH values are only expected to range from 15-\n20%, however median peak gusts will be in the 20-25 mph range across \nSE Wyoming. Further, fair overnight recoveries of 50-60% are \nexpected, keeping us below elevated fire weather criteria. \n\nA similar pattern will remain in place on Sunday with above-average \ntemperatures and mostly dry conditions expected. Still, little to no \nconcern for fire weather as minimum RH values will stay well-above \n20%. Long-range ensemble guidance is hinting at another influx of \nmonsoonal moisture being advected via SW flow on the western \nperiphery of the ridge, with PWAT values ranging from the 90th-95th \npercentile relative to climatology. Deterministic runs show little \nin the way of any synoptic-scale forcing for ascent to actually \nutilize this anomalous moisture and translate it to precipitation at \nthe surface at this time, unfortunately. \n\nMonday looks to be a bit more active than over the weekend as we \nmaintain the supply of monsoonal moisture out of the southwest. \nNAEFS guidance has PWAT ranging from 2-4 sigma at this time, with \nour region situated ahead a somewhat amplified positively-tilted \nshortwave trough over the Great Basin. This should provide at least \nsome forcing for showers and thunderstorms heading during the day. \nThe threat for any heavy rain is uncertain at this time as the \nforcing isn't anticipated to be all that widespread, and although \nPWAT will be on the high end of the distribution, we are getting to \nthat time of year where widespread and prolonged forcing gives us \nour most beneficial moisture, rather than a single thunderstorm with \nextreme rainfall rates that manages to sit over a localized area. \nUnsurprisingly due to more moisture and therefore clouds/potential \nprecipitation, highs on Monday will be a bit cooler, though still a \nfew degrees above average. Although exact timing is highly uncertain \nat this range, a cold front looks to push across SE WY and the \nNebraska Panhandle sometime during the day on Monday, which will \ndecrease our temperatures as we head into the middle of next week. \n\nThe shortwave trough skirts across the area on Tuesday, placing us \nin more of a WNW flow regime. This will shunt off our supply of \nsubtropical moisture leaving us with drier conditions, especially in \nthe cooler, post-frontal air mass. Maybe our first actual taste of \nfall that is long overdue? We shall see...",
